<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_for.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-for">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-for">
  <primary>FOR</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>FOR</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>FOR</refname>
  <refpurpose>expand a list into one row per element</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
FOR <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> IN <replaceable class="parameter">list</replaceable> [ WITH OFFSET [ AS <replaceable class="parameter">offset_variable</replaceable> ] ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-for-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>FOR</command> joins each input row with the elements of a list. An input row that
   meets a list of four elements becomes four output rows. When the list depends on the row,
   the expansion runs again for every row.
  </para>

  <para>
   The element variable has to be a new name. Reusing a name that is in scope raises
   <literal>duplicate variable "&lt;name&gt;"</literal>. <literal>WITH OFFSET</literal> binds
   the position of each element, and the first position is 0.
  </para>

  <para>
   An empty list, a null list and a missing property all produce zero rows, so a
   <command>FOR</command> over nothing drops its input row. A scalar is not a list. It is
   refused rather than wrapped in a list of one.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>FOR</command> may open a query, sit in the middle of one, or drive a write clause.
   It cannot end one.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-for-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name bound to one element per output row. It must not already be in scope.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">list</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Any expression that produces a list: a literal, <function>range()</function>, a property
      holding a list, a list built by an earlier <function>collect()</function>, or two lists
      concatenated with <literal>+</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">offset_variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name bound to the position of the element, counting from 0. Leave the
      <literal>AS</literal> clause out and the position is bound to <literal>offset</literal>
      instead. <literal>offset</literal> is a reserved word, so every later reference to it has
      to be written <literal>"offset"</literal>. Prefer the <literal>AS</literal> form.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-for-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Expand a list of titles and look each one up:
<programlisting>
FOR t IN ['The Matrix', 'Inception', 'Gattaca']
MATCH (m:movie {title: t})
RETURN t AS title, m.year AS year ORDER BY year;

    title     | year
--------------+------
 "Gattaca"    | 1997
 "The Matrix" | 1999
 "Inception"  | 2010
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <xref linkend="cypher-unwind"/> expands the same list and returns the same rows:
<programlisting>
UNWIND ['The Matrix', 'Inception', 'Gattaca'] AS t
MATCH (m:movie {title: t})
RETURN t AS title, m.year AS year ORDER BY year;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>WITH OFFSET AS</literal> names the position. Use it when the order of the list is
   part of the answer, as in a ranked list of titles:
<programlisting>
FOR t IN ['The Matrix', 'Inception', 'Gattaca'] WITH OFFSET AS i
MATCH (m:movie {title: t})
RETURN i, t AS title ORDER BY i;

 i |    title
---+--------------
 0 | "The Matrix"
 1 | "Inception"
 2 | "Gattaca"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
   The first element is at 0, not at 1.
  </para>

  <para>
   Leaving the <literal>AS</literal> clause out still binds the position, but under the name
   <literal>offset</literal>, which is a reserved keyword. Every later reference has to be
   quoted:
<programlisting>
FOR t IN ['The Matrix', 'Inception'] WITH OFFSET RETURN "offset", t;

 offset |      t
--------+--------------
 0      | "The Matrix"
 1      | "Inception"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Drop the quotes and the query does not parse:
<programlisting>
FOR t IN ['The Matrix'] WITH OFFSET RETURN offset, t;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near "offset"
LINE 1: FOR t IN ['The Matrix'] WITH OFFSET RETURN offset, t;
                                                   ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The list can come from the matched row, in which case the expansion runs once per row:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
FOR e IN m.embedding
RETURN e ORDER BY e;

      e
-------------
 -0.11921061
 -0.07594558
 0.04081754
 0.29592122
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <function>range()</function> is a list like any other, so <command>FOR</command> can drive a
   lookup over generated keys:
<programlisting>
FOR y IN range(1999, 2001)
MATCH (m:movie {year: y})
RETURN y, m.title AS film ORDER BY y, film;

  y   |     film
------+--------------
 1999 | "The Matrix"
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-for-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>FOR</command> compiles to a lateral <function>unnest()</function>, while
   <command>UNWIND</command> compiles to a <literal>ProjectSet</literal>. The two clauses mean
   the same thing, but they are not the same plan:
<programlisting>
EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF) FOR x IN [1, 2, 3] RETURN x;

                 QUERY PLAN
--------------------------------------------
 Function Scan on unnest _agens_default_for
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Because the expansion goes through <function>unnest()</function>, a scalar source is a type
   error rather than a list of one element. An integer raises
   <literal>function unnest(integer) does not exist</literal> and a scalar property raises
   <literal>cannot extract elements from a scalar</literal>. A null is different. It is treated
   as an empty list and yields no rows.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>for</literal> is already a reserved keyword in
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>, so the clause puts no new restriction on identifier
   names. <literal>offset</literal> is reserved as well, which is why the bare
   <literal>WITH OFFSET</literal> form has to be quoted where the position is read back.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-for-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>FOR</command> implements the GQL FOR statement of ISO/IEC 39075:2024, section
   14.8, feature GQ10, "FOR statement: list value support", and feature GQ24, "FOR statement:
   WITH OFFSET". Expansion of a list value expression, evaluation once per input row, a null
   source treated as an empty list, and the position counting from 0 that section 14.8 general
   rule 2 specifies are all supported. <literal>WITH ORDINALITY</literal>, feature GQ11, which
   counts from 1, is not. Add one to the offset instead. A binding table reference as the
   source, feature GQ23, is not supported either, because AgensGraph has no named binding
   tables. One deviation remains: the standard writes the position variable without
   <literal>AS</literal>, as <literal>WITH OFFSET i</literal>, while AgensGraph requires
   <literal>WITH OFFSET AS i</literal> and defaults a bare <literal>WITH OFFSET</literal> to the
   name <literal>offset</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher spells this clause <literal>UNWIND ... AS x</literal>, which AgensGraph accepts
   unchanged and which has no offset form.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

